> My thoughts:

> 1. Do not allow nymphs to get into melee range with you. I know the
> temptation is great for melee monsters like Valkyrie and Samurai, but
> ranged attacks (projectiles, spells, wands, thrown potions) really
> have their place in every character's journey to ascension. Or even
> let your pet deal with them.

Or even just ignore them.  Most nymphs are created sleeping.  If
not: a Samurai starts with a bow and arrows---in early game, I
use these mainly on nymphs and floating eyes.  Nymphs are, in my
experience, extremely weak characters, and two or three arrows
should suffice.  (Valks can do the same with daggers.  Although
they only start with one, by the time they reach level 9, they
should have at least 3 or 4, probably more.)

I might add that a Samurai is always legal, so by level 9, he
should have had Excalibur.  Which means that a nymph will
normally be one hit in melee.  Between that and a Samurai's
natural speed, it should be possible to melee a nymph and kill
her before she's had time to steal anything.  (IMHO, it's not
the preferred way.  But if one is created, awake, immediately
next to you, it's probably better than taking a turn to switch
to the bow.)

And as for the irony of the RGN: I still remember a game as a
Knight where a nymph was created awake, immediately next to me,
in minetown (something like level 6 in that game).  Since she
only stole an unidentified wand that I'd picked up before
entering the mines, I didn't worry about it.  I noted that she
was on the level, and figured I'd get it back later.  (I did
have a stack of daggers, but I was still unskilled with them.)
Well, sure enough, a little later, she showed up in perfect
position, about 6 squares off in a straight corridor: perfect
for my ranged attack.  And that's how I learned that the RGN had
generously given my a wand of death in the very early levels,
which I just hadn't identified yet.

> 2. Go back up when your AC gets too high. Find some temporary armor to
